## Summary

On Windows, calling `process.stdin.setRawMode(true)` overwrites all existing console mode flags instead of preserving them. This breaks mouse input in TUI applications because `ENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT` and `ENABLE_EXTENDED_FLAGS` get wiped out.

## Reproduction

```javascript
// 1. Set console mode with mouse input enabled (via native addon or FFI)
SetConsoleMode(stdin, ENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT | ENABLE_EXTENDED_FLAGS | ...)
// stdin mode is now 0x03b7

// 2. Enable raw mode
process.stdin.setRawMode(true)
// stdin mode is now ~0x0200 - mouse flags are gone!
```

## Test Case

Using a native FFI call to demonstrate (tested with Bun, but the same issue exists in Node.js):

```javascript
const { execSync } = require('child_process');

// Before setRawMode - set mouse input flags via PowerShell/native call
// ... (application sets ENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT | ENABLE_EXTENDED_FLAGS)

// Enable raw mode - this clobbers the flags
process.stdin.setRawMode(true);

// Mouse input no longer works because flags were overwritten
```

The corruption happens within ~150ms of calling `setRawMode(true)`.

## Expected Behavior

`setRawMode(true)` should:
1. Read current console mode with `GetConsoleMode()`
2. Modify only the flags it needs to change for raw mode
3. Preserve existing flags like `ENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT` and `ENABLE_EXTENDED_FLAGS`

## Actual Behavior

`setRawMode(true)` calls `SetConsoleMode()` with a fixed set of flags, overwriting the entire console mode and wiping out any flags that were previously set by the application.

## Impact

This affects all TUI applications on Windows that need mouse input:
- Apps using Ink, Blessed, or similar TUI libraries  
- Terminal-based IDEs (like many AI coding assistants)
- Interactive CLI tools with mouse support

Known affected projects:
- OpenCode
- Various Ink-based TUI applications

## Environment

- OS: Windows 10/11
- Node.js version: All versions (the Windows console handling code has this pattern)
- Terminal: Windows Terminal / ConHost

## Suggested Fix

In the libuv or Node.js Windows TTY implementation:

```c
// Current approach (broken):
SetConsoleMode(handle, RAW_MODE_FLAGS);

// Fixed approach:
DWORD currentMode;
GetConsoleMode(handle, &currentMode);
DWORD newMode = (currentMode & ~FLAGS_TO_CLEAR) | FLAGS_TO_SET;
SetConsoleMode(handle, newMode);
```

This preserves existing flags while still enabling raw mode.

## Related

This same issue likely exists in libuv's Windows TTY implementation, which Node.js uses.

