## Real-world impact via body-parser

This issue was documented by Gareth Heyes at PortSwigger Research in their article on server-side prototype pollution (https://portswigger.net/research/server-side-prototype-pollution).

The article demonstrates a practical exploit chain where this `_addHeaderLine` behavior enables charset injection attacks. Here's how it works:

### The gadget in body-parser

The `body-parser` module uses this code to determine charset:

```javascript
function getCharset (req) {
  try {
    return (contentType.parse(req).parameters.charset || '').toLowerCase()
  } catch (e) {
    return undefined
  }
}

read(req, res, next, parse, debug, {
  encoding: charset,  // Uses the charset from getCharset()
  inflate: inflate,
  limit: limit,
  verify: verify
})
```

### The attack flow

1. Attacker pollutes `Object.prototype['content-type']` with value `'application/json; charset=utf-7'` (via unsafe JSON merge elsewhere in the application)
2. Client sends a request with legitimate `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` header
3. Node's `_addHeaderLine` sees `dest['content-type'] !== undefined` (due to prototype pollution) and **drops the real header**
4. The polluted `content-type: 'application/json; charset=utf-7'` is inherited via prototype chain
5. `body-parser` parses the request body using UTF-7 instead of UTF-8
6. Attacker can inject UTF-7 encoded payloads (e.g., `+AGIAYQByAC0` decodes to `bar`) that bypass input validation

### Quote from the research

> "After I wrote this up Andrzej Matykiewicz (a colleague) pointed out that the code above (another code snippet)  doesn't explain why even a request that explicitly sets the UTF-8 charset in the content-type header still gets parsed using UTF-7 once we pollute the prototype... We then spent some time trying to understand why this works. After looking through loads of lines of code and spending a lot of time with the debugger in devtools, we found out that some Node code is actually responsible for removing the content-type from the request object... So Node only copies the header if it is undefined... When a content type property is available to inherit via the prototype chain, this won't be undefined. Therefore, Node thinks this property already exists on the destination object and doesn't add it. This then explains why the prototype pollution works: the property representing the actual content-type header is effectively removed from the request headers, which enables the inherited property to be used instead... It would be better to check if it's an own property using the hasOwnProperty() method"

